\description{
Implements Cheong's (2007) Multivariate Beta Binomial Distribution (MBD).
Vehicle co-exposure probabilities for pairs (observed) and for three or more
vehicles (imputed from a Beta-Binomial fitted to the subset's own mean
audience and mean pairwise duplication) are combined into the full
\eqn{2^m} joint zero/one exposure grid by Waring's (1792) inclusion-exclusion
theorem. Vehicles are then peeled off one at a time, in reverse aggregation
order: each remaining exposure pattern's row is expanded from a zero/one
state into the peeled vehicle's own insertion-level Beta-Binomial
distribution by a row-specific conditional allocation, and the two component
distributions are convolved into a growing pseudo-vehicle.
}
\details{
\code{calc_mbd()} (Cheong, 2007) is a different procedure from
\code{\link[=fit_bbd_to_reach]{fit_bbd_to_reach()}}, which fits
one Beta-Binomial to an externally given reach. MBD is the \emph{Multivariate}
Beta Binomial Distribution of several vehicles described here, whereas
\code{\link[=fit_bbd_to_reach]{fit_bbd_to_reach()}} fits a single Beta-Binomial to a whole schedule.
}
\section{What is, and is not, guaranteed}{
Cheong's dissertation reports that the inclusion-exclusion grid can produce
small negative cell probabilities once four or more vehicles are combined,
because the co-exposure probability of three or more vehicles is \emph{imputed}
(no closed-form multivariate distribution stands behind it) rather than
observed. Cheong's four-vehicle example is fully corrected by a first-order
consistency check (every combined-exposure probability is shrunk to remain
below each of its immediate subsets); the five-vehicle example is not, and
the dissertation states that resolving this in general would need
increasingly complex higher-order checks that were never implemented,
proposing iterative proportional fitting as future work. \code{calc_mbd()}
therefore applies exactly the checks Cheong specifies (first order only)
and then, as a package safety net, sets any cell that is still negative in
the final collapsed distribution to zero and redistributes that mass
proportionally across the non-negative cells. This is reported in
\code{diagnostics}; a non-zero value means the result for that schedule relies on
this fallback rather than on a value Cheong verified as internally
consistent without it, and a zero value does not show that the result
conserves the marginals or the mean of the inputs (see "What the result
conserves" below). The function warns for four or more vehicles, the
range where Cheong's own checks are known to be insufficient by themselves.

This safety net is not Cheong's MBD-ADJ. Cheong (2007, Ch. 4.4) applies the
same zero-and-redistribute correction to the table that the dissertation
calls the UD (univariate distribution; one cell for each pattern of exposure
to the \code{m} vehicles, \code{2^m} cells), once it is formed and \emph{before} it is
expanded and collapsed, so that the later steps work with non-negative
values; here the
correction acts only on the final distribution, and only if it contains a
negative cell. The two procedures can give different results even when the
final distribution has no negative cell, so no result of this function
should be described as "MBD-ADJ", and the empirical evidence for MBD-ADJ
does not transfer to it. The package does not implement the variants MBD,
MBD-ADJ and MBD-ADJ2 of the study as such (see the comparison of variants
below).

Cheong also reports that the aggregation order can change the collapsed
distribution, that this was not investigated systematically, and that the
model was evaluated on schedules of 12 vehicles because the computing time
of the exposure grid becomes prohibitive above 13 vehicles. \code{calc_mbd()}
stops above 12 vehicles for this reason.
}

\section{What the result conserves}{
Each vehicle enters the model with its own parameters (its Beta-Binomial
fitted from \code{R1} and \code{R2}), but the distribution that the model returns does
not necessarily reproduce the marginal distribution of each vehicle or the
mean number of exposures of the inputs, and this can happen with no negative
probability at all. The reason is the conditional allocation of the peeling
step, which is part of the published procedure. When a vehicle is expanded,
each row of the joint table is shared between the two expansions
\eqn{BB(\alpha, \beta + 1)} and \eqn{BB(\alpha + 1, \beta)} of that vehicle,
with weights \eqn{a = 1 - b} and \eqn{b = C (\alpha + \beta + 1) - \alpha},
where \eqn{C} is the conditional probability of exposure to the vehicle given
the row, obtained from the joint and marginal masses of the table. Cheong
(2007, pp. 65-66) does not use \eqn{C} as it comes: he bounds it to the
interval
\eqn{\alpha / (\alpha + \beta + 1) \le C \le (\alpha + 1) / (\alpha + \beta + 1)}
that the Beta-Binomial marginal of the vehicle allows. When the value implied
by the observed co-exposures falls outside the interval, replacing it by the
nearest end changes the conditional probability that the masses of the table
represent, and with it the marginals and the mean of the final distribution.
Cheong (2007, p. 90) acknowledges, for a different reason (the imputed
co-exposures of three or more vehicles), that the joint distributions of the
UD do not always add up to the marginals of each vehicle, and leaves the
correction, by iterative proportional fitting, for future research. The
mechanism described here is not the final safety net, which acts only on
negative cells, and it is not the expansion of \code{\link[=calc_cbd]{calc_cbd()}}, which expands
the remaining \eqn{n_i - 1} insertions of each vehicle conditionally on its
first exposure.

For example, two vehicles with two insertions each, \code{R1 = c(0.30, 0.20)},
\code{R2 = c(0.40, 0.30)} and a duplication of 0.15 between one insertion of each
(the example below) are compatible data: a joint distribution of the numbers
of exposures of the two vehicles exists with those audiences, reaches after
two insertions and duplications, and the duplication (0.15) is above the
random one (0.06). The inputs imply \eqn{2 \times 0.30 + 2 \times 0.20 = 1}
exposure per person. The distribution returned by \code{calc_mbd()} has no
negative cell (\code{negative_mass_adjusted} is 0) and a mean of 1.0538, a
\code{mean_error} of +0.0538, because the conditional probability of one of the
expansions is bounded from 0.0714 to 0.125 and that of another from 0.75 to
0.6667. The result is a reach of 49.59\% and an average frequency of 2.125,
whereas the contacts of the input per person reached would be 2.017.

Therefore, always read \code{gross_mean_contacts}, \code{distribution_mean_contacts} and
\code{mean_error}, whether or not \code{negative_mass_adjusted} is zero. The
\code{average_frequency} of the result is the mean of the returned distribution
divided by its reach, and it must not be combined with the contacts or the
gross rating points of the inputs as if the mean had not changed: the
distribution of the example implies about 105.4 rating points, not the 100 of
the inputs, and the identity between rating points, reach and average
frequency requires both quantities to be computed from the same
distribution. With a binomial-limit vehicle the two expansions coincide and
the allocation has no effect on that vehicle.
}

\section{Validation against the published example}{
For the three-vehicle example of Cheong (2007, p. 75), which Cheong,
Leckenby and Eakin (2011, Table 4) also publish, the largest absolute
difference between this function and the printed seven-cell distribution is
0.0020348568 in probability (0.20348568 percentage points), in the cell of
three exposures. The regression test accepts differences below 0.0025: that
is a threshold above the real maximum, not an estimate of the error of the
function. The rounding of one printed cell to three decimals (at most
0.0005) is smaller than that maximum. The difference of every cell is
recorded in the installed file \code{DATA-PROVENANCE.md}. This is an approximate
replication of one printed example, not an independent validation of the
model's predictions. The printed intermediate tables contain arithmetic
inconsistencies (a product printed as .024(.057) = .003, which is .0014; a
sum of Beta-Binomial parameters of 7.294, where the printed formula gives
7.543; an exclusive-grid sum of 1.001). They are a plausible explanation for
some of the differences, but their full causal attribution has not been
established: the public tests do not include an independent implementation
of the printed procedure.
In the binomial limit of a vehicle's own distribution (\code{R2} equal to the
reach under independence), the conditional distributions of the peeling step
coincide, so the result of this model does not depend on the duplication
between vehicles in that limit; this follows from Cheong's construction.
}

\section{Published evidence belongs to the study's variants}{
Of the eleven models Cheong evaluated (comScore 2003 data, 440 schedules;
Cheong, 2007, Table 6.2.1), the three versions of MBD were the most accurate
for reach alone, but not for the complete exposure distribution. The figures
below are the study's, for its own implementations of each version, not
measurements of this package:
\tabular{lrr}{
Model of the study \tab AER (percent) \tab APE (percent)\cr
MBD \tab 1.34 \tab 10.19\cr
MBD-ADJ \tab 1.40 \tab 12.10\cr
MBD-ADJ2 \tab 1.18 \tab 11.83\cr
CANEX \tab 1.69 \tab 6.91\cr
CBD \tab 1.60 \tab 8.80
}
The best result for reach (1.18\%) belongs to MBD-ADJ2, not to a plain MBD,
and not to the safety net of this function, so it must not be read as the
expected error of \code{\link[=calc_mbd]{calc_mbd()}}. For the complete distribution, \code{\link[=calc_canex]{calc_canex()}}
and the Conditional Beta Distribution model (\code{\link[=calc_cbd]{calc_cbd()}}) were more accurate
than all the MBD versions.
}

\section{Computational limits}{

The work of the schedule is estimated from the insertions before anything
is allocated, and a schedule above the budget is refused with an
explanation. The budget is the option \code{mediaPlanR.max_work}, \code{1e9}
elementary operations by default (one to two minutes); see the section
"Computational limits" of \link{mediaPlanR}.
}

\section{Domain of validity}{

In plain words. These models take the overlaps between vehicles
(the duplications) from your data and use them to build the whole
distribution of exposures. For that to be possible the overlaps you supply
must be possible, and plausible together:
\itemize{
\item Two vehicles cannot overlap more than the smaller of their
audiences, nor less than their audiences force them to (two audiences of
60\% of the population must overlap by at least 20\%). If a duplication
breaks these limits, the function stops with an error naming the pair:
the data cannot be right and should be reviewed.
\item The overlaps of three vehicles must also fit together. If they
cannot, the function stops with an error naming the three vehicles.
\item The studies that evaluated these models mostly used vehicles that
overlap at least as much as chance alone would produce, that is, at
least the product of their audiences (two vehicles with audiences of 30\%
and 20\% overlap by 6\% if people chose vehicles independently). A smaller
overlap is not impossible and is not necessarily a measurement error: it
can be valid data. But when a pair overlaps clearly less than that, the
approximations of the models can differ a lot from one model to another,
and the function issues a warning that names the pair. Check how the
data were defined and read the diagnostics; do not replace valid
observations automatically.
}
An error has one of two causes. Either the inputs break a constraint of the
data (impossible overlaps: correct them), or the inputs are possible but the
model's approximation cannot reproduce them (the third limit below: a valid
probabilistic construction can still be rejected by an approximate model).
Read the message to tell them apart, and do not change valid data only to
make a model run. A warning does not invalidate the calculation, but it asks
for caution: check the duplication of the pair named, which is often a
measurement problem (see below), and compare the result with another model.
Every diagnostic must be inspected before the result is interpreted; none
can be assumed to be harmless or, on the contrary, to invalidate the result.
The rest of this section gives the technical detail.

The multivariate models (\code{\link[=calc_canex]{calc_canex()}}, \code{\link[=calc_csd]{calc_csd()}}, \code{\link[=calc_msad]{calc_msad()}},
\code{\link[=calc_cbd]{calc_cbd()}} and \code{\link[=calc_mbd]{calc_mbd()}}) take the observed one-insertion duplications
as given and check them against the Fréchet bounds implied by the audiences
and, for every triple of vehicles, against joint feasibility. The published
methods have three limits that follow from this.

First, Hong (1998, pp. 238-239) formulates, for the models and the data of
his study, the hypothesis that the duplication between vehicles is not below
the random one, the product of the audiences, and treats null observed
duplications as measurement problems. It is not a condition of the data or
of the formulas: Danaher's canonical expansion itself admits negative
correlations (Danaher, 1991, equation 3), and the logical limits of a
duplication are the Fréchet bounds,
\eqn{\max(0, p_i + p_j - 1) \le p_{ij} \le \min(p_i, p_j)}. For example,
two vehicles with one insertion each and audiences of 0.30 and 0.20 can have
a duplication of 0.03, below the random 0.06, with the valid joint
distribution (neither, only the second, only the first, both) of
(0.53, 0.17, 0.27, 0.03), and \code{calc_canex()} reproduces it without negative
probabilities. With several insertions per vehicle, in contrast, a lower
duplication can make the approximations diverge considerably. The functions
warn (class \code{mediaPlanR_low_duplication}) when the canonical correlation of a
pair is below -0.1. That threshold is a decision of this package: the
published reference plans (Kim 2005, Appendix B, and Hong 1998, Appendix E;
80 plans) include pairs slightly below the random duplication, with
canonical correlations down to -0.071, and they raise no warning. For two vehicles with two insertions each, \code{R1 = 0.30} and
\code{R2 = 0.45}, a duplication of zero gives a reach of 0.90 under MSAD, 0.81
under MBD, 0.78 under CSD and CBD, and 0.79 under CANEX. Hong (1998,
pp. 238-239) treats a null duplication as a measurement error and replaces
it by \eqn{\hat{p}_{ij} = K' p_i p_j}, where \eqn{K'} is the ratio between observed
and random duplication over the pairs with non-zero observed duplication.
The package does not implement this imputation: \code{\link[=calc_agostini_duplication]{calc_agostini_duplication()}}
and \code{\link[=calc_hofmans_duplication]{calc_hofmans_duplication()}} estimate reach from a duplication matrix and
do not impute its elements. If duplications are imputed beforehand, the
result must be checked against the bounds described below. A null duplication
is not always a measurement error: it can also be a genuine observation.

Second, pairwise duplications that satisfy the Fréchet bounds can still be
impossible jointly for three or more vehicles. For every triple of vehicles
the functions check that the exposure to all three can have a non-negative
probability, given the audiences and the three pairwise duplications, and
stop with an error when it cannot. That condition is necessary but not
sufficient for four or more vehicles, for which the published methods check
feasibility at most to first order (Cheong 2007); \code{\link[=calc_mbd]{calc_mbd()}} and
\code{\link[=calc_cbd]{calc_cbd()}} zero any negative cell that remains, and \code{\link[=calc_canex]{calc_canex()}}
truncates it.

Third, a low duplication between vehicles with several insertions can make
the target of a sequential model invalid. For two vehicles with two
insertions each, \code{R1 = 0.5} and \code{R2 = 0.75}, \code{\link[=calc_csd]{calc_csd()}} and \code{\link[=calc_cbd]{calc_cbd()}}
stop with a duplication of zero, and \code{\link[=calc_msad]{calc_msad()}} with a duplication of zero
or 0.5, instead of altering an invalid target.
}

